Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - HALL - NIGHT

Period gothic live-action. In a narrow, gaslit hall, NARRATOR, a lean, pale man in his thirties with black hair, a severe face, and a black waistcoat, stands beside a closed bedroom door. A dark lantern hangs from his hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

NARRATOR places his fingers on the latch but does not yet turn it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! Yes, it was this!

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

MR. VALE, a frail, white-haired gentleman in a high-necked nightshirt, sleeps beneath a heavy bedcover. His shuttered room is black as pitch.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold.

At midnight, NARRATOR turns the latch a fraction and opens the door without a sound.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

NARRATOR slips the closed lantern through the narrow opening, then moves his head into the room with excruciating patience.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me—how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

Over seven long nights, NARRATOR repeats the ritual. The lantern opens by a hair. A single thin ray reaches MR. VALE'S face.

Each time, MR. VALE'S eyes remain closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAWN

Grey daylight seeps through the shutter cracks. NARRATOR enters boldly, now warm-faced and composed.

NARRATOR
Good morning, Mr. Vale. How have you passed the night?

MR. VALE looks up, unaware of the night visitor who has stood beside his bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The eighth midnight. NARRATOR eases the door inward more slowly than a watch's minute hand moves.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

NARRATOR suppresses a small chuckle.

MR. VALE jolts awake in bed.

MR. VALE
Who's there?

NARRATOR freezes in the doorway. The lantern remains closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down.

MR. VALE sits rigidly in the dark, listening toward the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was still sitting up in the bed listening; just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

A slight groan rises from MR. VALE.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no! It was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

MR. VALE'S hands clutch the bedcover. He strains to hear the chimney, the floor, the room itself.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had been trying to fancy his fears causeless. “It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor.” But he found all in vain.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim.

After a long wait, NARRATOR opens a minute crevice in the lantern.

A dim ray, thin as a spider's thread, falls full upon MR. VALE'S left eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

The pale blue eye stares through its hideous veil. Nothing else of MR. VALE'S face catches the ray.

A low, dull, quick sound begins beneath the bedding, like a watch enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

NARRATOR holds the lantern motionless. His breath stops.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

Minutes pass. MR. VALE trembles beneath the fixed beam. The muffled heartbeat rises until it seems to fill the room.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At the dead hour of the night, amid the dreadful silence of that old house, so strange a noise as this excited me to uncontrollable terror. Yet, for some minutes longer, I refrained and stood still.

NARRATOR'S eyes dart toward the wall shared with a neighbour.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

NARRATOR throws open the lantern and leaps into the room with a yell.

MR. VALE shrieks once—once only.

NARRATOR drags MR. VALE to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

The covered lantern trembles on the floor. Beneath the bed, the heart continues its muffled beating.

NARRATOR kneels beside the bed, listening.

At last, the sound ceases.

NARRATOR removes the bed and presses his palm to MR. VALE'S chest.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was no pulsation. He was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

By lantern light, NARRATOR dismembers MR. VALE'S corpse. A wooden tub catches every drop.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

NARRATOR lifts three planks from the floor and deposits the remains between the scantlings.

He replaces the boards with exacting care. The seams vanish beneath his fingertips.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that.

Four bell chimes sound in the distance.

A hard knock strikes the street door.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - HALL - PRE-DAWN

NARRATOR descends with a light step and opens the street door.

Three police officers enter: OFFICER REYNOLDS, sturdy and grey-whiskered in a dark wool coat; OFFICER HALE, tall and narrow-faced beneath his brimmed hat; and OFFICER MORGAN, younger and clean-shaven, carrying a leather notebook.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play had been aroused.

NARRATOR smiles easily at the officers.

NARRATOR
The shriek was my own in a dream. Mr. Vale is absent in the country.

NARRATOR gestures through the house, inviting the officers inside.

NARRATOR
Search—search well.

NARRATOR leads REYNOLDS, HALE, and MORGAN toward the bedroom stairs.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The officers inspect the room. MR. VALE'S treasures remain secure and undisturbed.

NARRATOR brings in four wooden chairs. He places his own chair directly over the repaired floorboards.

OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER HALE, and OFFICER MORGAN sit. They chat of familiar things while NARRATOR answers cheerily.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

NARRATOR'S smile thins. His cheek loses its colour.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

A faint, cotton-muffled ticking begins beneath NARRATOR'S chair.

NARRATOR talks faster, filling the room with harmless words. The three officers continue their pleasant conversation.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I talked more freely to get rid of the feeling; but it continued and gained definiteness—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

The ticking grows louder.

NARRATOR rises and argues about trifles in a high voice, gesticulating violently.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not. I talked more quickly—more vehemently; but the noise steadily increased.

NARRATOR paces across the floor with heavy strides. The officers watch him, politely puzzled.

NARRATOR swings his chair up and grates its legs across the boards.

The ticking rises above the scrape. Loud. Louder. Louder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew! They were making a mockery of my horror!

NARRATOR wheels on the officers, wild-eyed and shaking.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

NARRATOR points at the floor. OFFICER REYNOLDS kneels beside the seams between the planks.

> FADE OUT.
